<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samarra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samarra is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, founded by the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a> as a new capital in the ninth century. It housed the caliph's Turkic slave-soldier army away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> describe the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchy-at-samarra">Anarchy at Samarra</a> (861–870), when four caliphs were murdered by their own troops, as the point at which military domination of the caliphate became irreversible.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samarra.jpg" alt="Samarra" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abdulmomen Laith Mahmoud (عبدالمؤمن ليث محمود) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%B5%D9%88%D8%B1%D9%87_%D9%81%D9%8A_%D8%A7%D8%AC%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%A1_%D9%85%D8%B7%D8%B1%D9%8A%D9%87_%D9%84%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A3%D8%B0%D9%86%D9%87_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%84%D9%88%D9%8A%D9%87_%D9%81%D9%8A_%D8%B3%D8%A7%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%82.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Samarra stands on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in the Saladin Governorate, 125 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Its earliest remains were first excavated between 1911 and 1914 by the German archaeologists Ernst Herzfeld and Friedrich Sarre, and Samarra became the type site of the Samarra culture, which flourished alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period">Ubaid period</a> as one of the first town states in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. A Neo-Assyrian city, Sur-marrati, refounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> in 690 BCE, is insecurely identified with a fortified site opposite the modern city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 836 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a> founded a new capital on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. He built extensive palace complexes surrounded by garrison settlements for his guards, drawn mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, chiefly the Turks, or from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Although often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> slave soldiers, their status was quite elevated, and some of their commanders bore Sogdian titles of nobility. Earlier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> had dug a canal here and laid out the octagonal city of al-Mubarak, abandoned unfinished in 796.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mutawakkil sponsored lavish palaces, such as al-Mutawakkiliyya, and the Great Mosque of Samarra with its spiral minaret, the Malwiya, built in 847. The Nestorian patriarch Sargis moved the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to Samarra to be close to the seat of power. Samarra remained the caliph's residence until 892, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> returned the capital to Baghdad. The city was looted around this time, and its population probably fell, though it remained an important market centre. Later the river's course shifted to the east, the main road from Baghdad to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> moved to the west bank, and Samarra lost its importance as a trading town.</p><p>From the tenth century the city became a pilgrimage site. In the 1730–1735 Ottoman–Persian War the Battle of Samarra cost over 50,000 Ottoman and Persian casualties and decided the fate of Ottoman Iraq, which stayed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>'s suzerainty until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. In the 1950s the Samarra Barrage created Lake Tharthar to prevent the flooding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Samarra is a holy site in Shia Islam because of the al-Askari Shrine, which holds the tombs of the tenth and eleventh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imams</a>, Ali al-Hadi and Hasan al-Askari, and of Hakimah and Narjis, female relatives of the Prophet and the Imams. The archaeological site preserves much of the historic city's plan, architecture and artistic relics, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2007. A story of an appointment with Death at Samarra, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Babylonian Talmud</a>, was retold by W. Somerset Maugham and gave John O'Hara's novel its title.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samarra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He intended to give the caliph a loyal personal force independent of Arab and Khurasani factions, and moved the capital to <b>Samarra</b> in 836 to house it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the north the Ubaid followed the Hassuna, <b>Samarra</b> and Halaf cultures and a short Halaf-Ubaid Transitional period, and lasted only from about 5300 to 4300 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ubaid-culture">Ubaid culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Al-Ma'mun's successor al-Mu'tasim (r. 833–842) built a new force of Turkish slave soldiers from Central Asia, loyal to him personally rather than to the older Arab and Khurasani constituencies of the caliphate, and moved the court to a new capital at <b>Samarra</b> to house them.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>His viziers ran the government while he lived amid elaborate ceremony in the palaces of Baghdad, founded by al-Mansur in 762, and from 836 to 892 in <b>Samarra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abbasids executed at <b>Samarra</b> 42 Byzantine officers and notables, taken hostage in the sack of Amorium in 838, after they refused to convert to Islam.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-6">6 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He was captured and executed in <b>Samarra</b> in 840, ending the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/qarinvand-dynasty">Qarinvand dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
